course: dictionary
lesson: 131
title: Adverbials and the fronted comma
level: primary-6
stars: 2
explain: An adverbial is a word, phrase or clause that does the job of an adverb. It tells us how, when, where or how often something happens: «in the morning», «after lunch», «with a smile». Ask yourself: does this part tell me when, where, how or how often?
explain: The adjective «adverbial» describes anything that works like an adverb, so an adverbial phrase is a phrase doing an adverb's job. An adverbial can be one word, like «Suddenly,», a phrase, like «Under the bridge,», or a clause, like «When the bell rang,».
explain: When an adverbial comes at the start of a sentence, it is a fronted adverbial, and it is usually followed by a comma: «Later that day, it snowed.» The comma shows the reader that the opening part has finished and the main sentence is about to begin.
fact: An adverbial tells when, where, how or how often something happens.
fact: A fronted adverbial is an adverbial placed at the start of a sentence.
fact: A fronted adverbial is usually followed by a comma.
fact: Fronted adverbials of place include «Under the bridge,», «In the garden,» and «Far away,».
fact: Starting sentences in different ways makes your writing more interesting to read.
exercise: In «Later that day, it snowed.», which part is the fronted adverbial? | *Later that day | snowed. | it snowed | Later
exercise: Which sentence puts the comma in the right place? | After, school we went to the park. | After school we went, to the park. | *After school, we went to the park.
exercise: What does an adverbial tell us? | *When, where, how or how often something happens | Whether a word is a verb | What a noun is called | How many words are in a sentence
test: Which opening is a fronted adverbial of place? | *In the garden, | Brightly, | Happy, | Quickly,
test: Where should the comma go in «After lunch we played football»? | *After lunch, we played football. | After lunch we, played football. | After, lunch we played football.
exam: Which opening is a clause used as an adverbial? | Quickly, | Suddenly, | *When the bell rang, | Under the bridge,
